Vipi is an independent application and is not affiliated with Signal.

On-device language identification and full-message translation use Google ML Kit.
Its SDKs and platform-managed model preparation are governed by their respective
terms; inference runs locally. Retained upstream CLD2 license copies describe
historical source included in the corresponding-source archive, not the current
runtime language identifier.

Offline speech fallback uses eSpeak NG core 699e79690f23e2558d990a3a78b0050745b96932
(GPL-3.0-or-later), its included Unicode/BSD/Apache components, and Sonic
9a8d05dc0baa9159fc322dd9905a04e23b161337 (Apache-2.0). Original source, licenses
and per-file provenance are retained in android/vendor. Data is regenerated by
scripts/build-android-speech-data.py; JNI and integration source are included.

Mandarin pronunciation data in Vipi’s compiled eSpeak NG dictionary incorporates
Unihan kMandarin readings from the Unicode Consortium and compound readings from
CC-CEDICT contributors, with corrections incorporated by eSpeak NG, including
contributions from the Ekho project. The historical CC-CEDICT data was offered
under Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 3.0 Unported.
Historical dictionary terms: https://cc-cedict.org/wiki/start?rev=1324464519
License: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/

Verbatim dependency notices are packaged in assets/legal/third-party, with
resolved release artifact hashes and original notice paths in index.json.
Pinned upstream license copies are in assets/legal/upstream. The app exposes
these through Settings > Licenses and notices. Regenerate the artifact inventory
with scripts/android-dependency-inventory.gradle and package notices with
scripts/collect-android-notices.py when dependencies change.

A complete corresponding-source release and final combined-distribution review
remain required before public distribution. This file does not relicense any
